# s3-26: Computer Use desktop wiring (applied by DESKTOP-WIRE-G4, which deletes this file)

No HOST_METHOD row and no protocol row: every `host/computer/*` handler names its own method constant and is served by name
(HOST_CAPABILITIES.computer already says so). SERVICE_PENDING: not touched (host methods are served by the desktop).

Built here: host/register-s3.ts `registerComputerHost(connection, wiring: ComputerHostWiring): () => void` (registers the capture,
displays, foregroundLease, internalWindows, pointerFeedback, cursorPrepare, appWindows, appInput, pixels, permissions and command
handlers; draws the arrow of appInput through the presence mirror; reports lock / suspend / display transitions as
`computer/userControl` takeover while `coreDrivesComputer(mirror)`, bound once `ready` resolves). computer-user-control.ts:
`createComputerPillControls({ core, mirror, host?, abortSession, onError? })` (option `snapshot` is now `mirror: { backend, snapshot }`;
`host` is optional: absent, every control goes to the core), `createCoreUserControl().configureIdleResume(seconds)`.

## 1. main/host/index.ts (DesktopHostOptions, createDesktopHost)

  import { registerComputerHost, type ComputerHostWiring } from './register-s3';

  - Remove the options `computer`, `computerAppInput`, `computerPermissions`, `computerCommand` and the imports of
    `registerComputerHost`/`ComputerControlSource` ('./computer'), `ComputerAppInputDeps`, `registerComputerCommand`,
    `registerComputerPermissions`; add `computer: ComputerHostWiring`.
  - Replace the three calls (registerComputerHost(connection, options.computer, { appInput: ... }), registerComputerPermissions,
    registerComputerCommand) by
      const stopComputer = registerComputerHost(connection, options.computer);
    and call `stopComputer()` in the connection.onClose listener beside tabs.stop().
  - In host/computer.ts the exported function of the same name that register-s3.ts imports under the alias registerComputerHandlers
    may be renamed `registerComputerHandlers` once the import in index.ts is gone (register-s3.ts imports it as
    `{ registerComputerHost as registerComputerHandlers }`: change that line and the export together).

## 2. main/protocol/desktop-host-wiring.ts (createLocalHostRole), in the same change

  The mirror (`computerPill`) must exist before the host does. Create it first (the createComputerPillSource(Date.now, {...}) block,
  bindPointerPresence and watchSection stay as they are, moved above createDesktopHost), then
      const host = createDesktopHost({ ...options, connection: createHostConnection(protocol), computer: { ...computer, mirror: computerPill } });
  with `LocalHostRoleOptions extends Omit<DesktopHostOptions, 'connection' | 'computer'>` plus `computer: Omit<ComputerHostWiring, 'mirror'>`
  (destructured out of the options beside protocol and onClaimFailed). While the TS host exists the mirror keeps
  `coordinator: computerUseCoordinator` (it delegates only while the section names `desktopHost` as the backend).

## 3. main/index.ts

  a. createLocalHostRole options: delete `computer`, `computerAppInput`, `computerPermissions`, `computerCommand`; add
       computer: {
         permissions: { read: readComputerPermissions, request: requestComputerPermissions },
         environment: {
           ready: app.whenReady(),
           sources: () => ({ power: powerMonitor, screen }),
           onError: (error) => console.warn('[mixdog-computer] environment takeover report failed:', error),
         },
         legacyScreen: () => { const host = computerControlEnabled ? computerHost : null; return host ? { assertPublic: assertPublicComputerRequest, answer: (command, signal) => host.answerBridgeCommand(command as ComputerCommand, signal), displays: readDisplays } : null; },
         legacyCommand: () => { const host = computerControlEnabled ? computerHost : null; return host ? { assertPublic: assertPublicComputerRequest, answerBridgeCommand: (command, signal) => host.answerBridgeCommand(command as ComputerCommand, signal) } : null; },
       },
     (the two getters are the bodies of the current `computer` and `computerCommand` options, unchanged).
     Delete `bindCoreComputerEnvironment`, `unbindComputerEnvironment`, their call (`bindCoreComputerEnvironment(localProtocol)`, ~line 3038)
     and `unbindComputerEnvironment()` (~line 3168), and the imports of `bindComputerEnvironment`.
  b. createComputerPillControls call (installLocalWindowFeatures): `snapshot: () => hostRole.computerPill.snapshot()` becomes
     `mirror: hostRole.computerPill`; `host: {...}` stays while the TS host exists and is passed only when it does.
  c. TS host created only when it is used: `createComputerHost` runs when the setting `desktop.computerBackend` is `desktopHost`
     (windowsWorker 'powershell') or while the dual path's legacy screen capture still needs it; with the Rust helper as the backend
     none is created (`computerHost` stays null; `createComputerPillControls` gets no `host`; `computerHost?.` uses already tolerate null).
  d. computerUseOnWindow (installLocalWindowFeatures): `computerUseOperates(hostRole.computerPill, electronWindowForNativeId, window)`
     (the mirror's windowIdsWithActiveCommand: the section's windows for the helper, the coordinator's for the desktop host).
  e. `computerAppInput.showCursor` is gone with item 3a (register-s3.ts draws it through the mirror).
  f. s3-15 desktop section: drop `applyGnomeExtensionSetting` and its import from applyComputerControlSetting (the core does it) and
     delete computer/host/gnome-extension.ts and gnome-extension.test.mjs.

## 4. Not done here (needs the initialize answer on the host connection, which host/connection.ts does not carry)

  `ComputerHostWiring.pixels.blobChunkBytes: () => limits.maxBlobPutChunkBytes`; without it every chunk is 96 KiB, under every core's limit.
